## Adding Conditions to Match Arms

A **guard** is an `if` condition added to a match arm. The arm only matches if BOTH the pattern matches AND the guard is true.

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let number = 15;

  let description = match number {
    n if n < 0 => "negative",
    n if n == 0 => "zero",
    n if n > 0 && n <= 10 => "small positive",
    n if n > 10 => "large positive",
    _ => "unknown",
  };

  io.println(description);
  return 0;
}
```

### Guard Syntax

```xiom
match value {
  pattern if condition => result,
  // ...
}
```

`pattern` captures the value (or matches a specific value), and `if condition` adds an extra check.

### Guards vs Pattern-Only

```xiom
// Without guard — only matches exact values
match x {
  0 => "zero",
  _ => "non-zero",
}

// With guard — can check properties of the value
match x {
  n if n % 2 == 0 => "even",
  n if n % 2 != 0 => "odd",
  _ => "unknown",
}
```

### Practical Examples

```xiom
// Classify numbers with guards
match score {
  s if s >= 90 => "A",
  s if s >= 80 => "B",
  s if s >= 70 => "C",
  s if s >= 60 => "D",
  _ => "F",
}

// Check multiple conditions
match age {
  a if a < 0 => "Invalid",
  a if a < 13 => "Child",
  a if a < 20 => "Teen",
  a if a < 65 => "Adult",
  _ => "Senior",
}
```

### Guard Limitations

The guard can reference the bound variable but can't modify it. Guards make `match` a complete replacement for `if/elif` chains in many cases.

